{
 "_comment": "Round 64, design 02 (b), rule in force: what every SDK's rule_in_force helper answers. document is a record bundle (its epa section's account_policy_gate and policy_gate) or a receipt answer (its receipt_context's account_policy and policy, and an attestation_gap_receipt when there is one); policy_history is null or a policy history as GET /policies/history or GET /account/policies/history returns it, oldest first. The answer lists each decision that names the policy change it was made under, the account's first: policy (account, key, or empty when nothing says which), activation_hash, policy_hash, fired_rule_id, outcome, found_by (record, receipt_context, receipt or policy_history), and sequence, chain_id and activated_at from the history when it holds that change (null otherwise). A refusal for missing fields (attestation_gap_receipt) gives one decision, outcome attestation_gap and fired_rule_id empty: the change it seals (round 64), else the change its receipt context names for the same policy, else, with a history, the last verdifax.policy.activation.v1 change activated at or before its gap_clock, compared to the nanosecond as instants, whose policy must be the refusal's; other history items are passed over. A sealed change its context names differently for the same policy, a change that was not the one in force at the clock when the history holds it, a needed history left out, and a document of neither shape are refused (expected_error). This mirrors verdifax-verify's gapcheck.go; the verifier stays the check. Every SDK reads this file in a test.",
